In this context, "Tratatul" refers to the Treaty on the Functioning of the European Union (TFEU), which is one of the two main treaties that form the legal basis of the European Union. The TFEU is significant as it sets out the powers and responsibilities of the European Union's institutions, including the European Commission, the Council of the European Union, and the member states. The role of the TFEU in shaping the framework within which these institutions operate is crucial and foundational.

The text states that only member states have the power to control the exercise of the Commission's executive competences. This means that the member states have the authority to ensure that the Commission carries out its tasks and responsibilities in accordance with the TFEU and other EU laws. The ability of member states to oversee the Commission's actions is a key aspect of the governance structure. The Council, on the other hand, does not have this power, and its role is limited to supporting the Commission in its work. The Council's supportive role underlines its position in the institutional hierarchy regarding the execution of competences.

This provision is important because it ensures that the Commission, as the EU's executive body, is accountable to the member states and that the member states have a say in the exercise of the Commission's powers. The accountability of the Commission to the member states is a fundamental principle that maintains the balance of power within the EU. It also helps to ensure that the Commission acts in the best interests of the EU as a whole, rather than solely in the interests of any one member state. This mechanism of control and accountability is vital for the functioning of the EU, as it promotes unity and coherence among the diverse member states, ensuring that the collective interests of the Union are prioritized.
