One of the most famous symptoms of leprosy is the loss of feeling in the hands and feet, which can lead to injuries and infections. This loss of sensation is well-known and frequently associated with the disease. However, it doesn't automatically cause body parts to fall off. In severe cases, long-term nerve damage can result in muscle weakness and the loss of limbs, but this is usually due to complications from the disease rather than being an automatic effect of the infection itself. The progression to such severe outcomes is generally the result of untreated or advanced cases of the disease, rather than an inevitable consequence of contracting the infection.

As for contagiousness, leprosy is generally considered to be a moderately contagious disease. It's transmitted through prolonged close contact with an infected person, typically through skin-to-skin contact or through shared living conditions. The disease has a long incubation period, often taking several months to years before symptoms appear. During this time, the person can unknowingly spread the bacteria to others. This prolonged incubation period means that a person may be carrying the bacteria without being aware and can potentially infect others without knowing it.

In conclusion, while leprosy can lead to various complications including the loss of limbs, it doesn't automatically cause body parts to fall off. Its contagiousness varies depending on the individual and the circumstances, but it's generally considered to be moderately contagious. This means that while the disease can be spread, it typically requires extended or close contact to do so.
